  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1201 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nToday and Tonight\n\nAn elevated warm front will move through the area this morning\nand surface warm front will move late this afternoon and\ntonight. Soundings continue to show that the deeper moisture and\nforcing will be found south of Interstate 90. This will result\nin showers (30-70 percent) and scattered storms from before\nsunrise into this morning. The 09.00z HREF mean and 24-hour\nlocalized probability-matched mean rainfall are only up to a\ntenth of an inch.\n\nHigh temperatures will range from the mid-60s to mid-70s. Low\ntemperatures will range from the  lower 50s to lower 60s.\n\nWeekend\n\nAn anomalously strong ridge of high pressure will provide dry \nweather and near- or record warmth. With the NBM high \ntemperature anomalies running on the cool side much of this \nweek, raised the NBM temperatures up by 2 to 4F by doing a 50-50\nblend of the NBM with the 90th NBM percentile. NAEFS has 850 mb\ntemperatures anomalies between 2.5 and 2.8 standard deviations \nabove normal. This will likely result in near- or record warmth.\n\nHigh temperatures will range from the upper 70s to mid-80s. Low\ntemperatures will range from the mid-50s to mid-60s.\n\nMonday through Wednesday\n\nA cold front will move through the area on Monday afternoon and\nnight. Soundings suggest that it will likely remain rather dry \nbelow 700 mb and that the low-levels may not become saturated \nalong the front until it moves east of the area. As result, kept\nrain chances from 15 to 25 percent.\n\nHigh temperatures on Monday will be in the 70s and then be in\n60s for Tuesday and lower to mid-60s on Wednesday. These\ntemperatures will be much closer to normal. Low temperatures will\nrange from the mid-40s to mid-50s on Monday night and from the\nmid-30s to around 50 on Tuesday night.",
